<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicola Pisano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1220–c. 1284</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicola Pisano was an Italian sculptor who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and is often seen as a founder of modern sculpture. His pulpits for the Pisa Baptistery, finished in 1260, and Siena Cathedral drew on Roman sarcophagi to give figures new weight and realism. With his son Giovanni he carved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontana-maggiore">Fontana Maggiore</a> in Perugia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicola-pisano.jpg" alt="Nicola Pisano" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pio Fedi (1816-1892), Italian sculptor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicola_Pisano,_by_Pio_Fedi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
